## Facts a caption must carry (each is in the sidecar `.json` too)
- **Scoring:** common target set (50 C/T splits, |T| = 109 of N = 328, stratified by task type; S chosen inside the pool C; error on the model's observed cells in T); floor = all of the model's observed C cells given, on the same T. The R_m∖S score is a labelled secondary and is not drawn in the main text.
- **Models drawn:** the headline subset `fixed` = models eligible at k_max = 109, the same at every k: G 154 models in 51 groups, T 13 models in 4 groups, S 22 in 11 (T and S are small; Table 2 says so in its header). All-eligible-per-k files are the supplement.
- **Intervals:** group bootstrap over organisations, B = 5000, 90% bounds. The random-k band in Fig. 1 is that bootstrap interval of the mean over models, not the draw-to-draw spread.
- **k\*(ε):** smallest grid k with excess over floor ≤ ε; the white diamond is the empirical k\*, the bar its 5th–95th bootstrap percentiles (grid values); a bar running to the axis end means the upper bound was never reached up to k = 109. ε = δ (0.10 points) and 2δ are not reached by the point curve (G), the margins 0.25 and 0.5 of the floor (1.17 and 2.34 points) are the secondary scale; no ε is presented as the right one.
- **Fig. 1 (c):** "best of S1, S2, S4 at each k" is chosen per k after seeing the gains, so it is an upper envelope of the simple selectors, not a rule.
- **T1:** the predicted curve is the design risk of the fitted factor model averaged over T; the figures show where it is above or below the measurement and say nothing more.
- **Fig. 3, synthetic panels (a, b):** standardised squared error, correct model (rung L0), 120 benchmarks, mean ± s.e. over 8 seeds; M = 320 (separated factors) and M = 240 (decaying spectrum); "T1, fitted parameters" is the prediction a user could compute, "true parameters" the one with the generating values (drawn in (b) only). Panel (c): estimation term = measured error minus the error with true parameters, separated factors, correct model; lines are the c/M least-squares fit per k, the log-log slopes of the fits lie between the two values printed in the panel. Panel (d) is in raw points² on the 154 G models (51 groups) and is a different unit from (a–c); the floor term does not depend on k (measured / T1 = 2.5 at k = 20; the same ratio is 7.9 on S, 22 models, and 9.6 on T, 13 models, in `floor_misfit_summary.csv`, not drawn); the T1 curve has no interval.
- **Fig. 4:** k\* is in units of each condition's own floor (ε = 0.25 and 0.5 × floor, the floor is panel (a) and moves with the condition); bounds are the 5th–95th bootstrap percentiles on the grid; the sweep grid ends at k = 32 (a bar running to the axis end would mean not reached, none does at these two ε; at ε = 0.1 × floor the reference is not reached, in `scope_ct_kstar.csv`). Hold-out rows (50 splits, models eligible at k = 109) and sweep rows (G folds 0–3 only: 67 models, 20 splits) are different samples, so the sweeps have their own reference row. Logit scores have their floor in logit units (0.38). Panel (d) applies the placement rule fixed before the real values were seen: a rung is consistent only if all six diagnostics of the real matrix lie inside its seed range (20 seeds); none is, so the real matrix is outside the ladder and no marker is put on a rung.
- **Table 3:** the files use 20 of the 50 shared C/T splits (experiment-repo's delivery note), the fixed k_max subset, so its floor (4.99 points on G) and the S6+P5 column differ slightly from Table 2 and Fig. 1 (50 splits; 5.06 and 5.1); delta_floor in these files is 1.15 (G), 1.06 (T), 1.04 (S). Rules: P0 benchmark mean (no k dependence; recorded under S0), P1 mean of revealed standardised scores, P2 ridge on the revealed benchmarks (trained on the EM-completed matrix), P3 kNN over training models, P4 Soft-Impute row update, P5 factor model, P6 principal-component regression with a sigmoid link (an adaptation, not as published). The difference column is RMSE(S6+P5) − RMSE(S6+rule), positive = the rule is better; up/down arrow = better/worse than P5 by more than δ_floor, no mark = matches or undecided at that margin (the verdict at δ_T is in the csv, not shown). Bold in the RMSE block marks the lowest rule in the row, not P5. S0 is not in the table (dropped for width; `table3_pipelines_ct.csv` has it).
- **δ_floor vs “0.25 floor”:** δ_floor is 0.25 × floor_j (the model's leave-one-out floor, aggregated over T; PREREG), about 0.23 × the mean floor RMSE printed beside it (1.17 against 5.06 on G). Table 2 and the margin lines of Fig. 1 are labelled δ_floor and 2δ_floor, no longer “0.25 fl.” / “0.5 fl.”. `ct_kstar.csv` now also has exact fractions of the mean floor RMSE (`frac_0.1/0.25/0.5`); the appendix k\* table prints both (“0.25 fl.” = the fraction) and the Fig. 4 hold-out rows use the fractions, as the sweep rows always did. They differ at some k\*: on G at half the floor k\* is 3 [3, 5]; at 2δ_floor it is 5 [3, 8].
- **Table 2b (Open LLM):** 4,507 models in 475 name-stem families (a heuristic; checkpoints of related models can sit in different groups), S = strongest quarter (1,127 models, 150 groups), no temporal split; scores are leaderboard sub-benchmark accuracies ×100, |T| = 12 stratified by sub-benchmark family, N = 39, k ∈ {1,2,3,5,8,13} (k/N up to 1/3); 50 shared C/T splits. The gap-based δ prints ~0 on this matrix, so δ_floor (1.13 on G, 1.11 on S) is the margin read; “equiv.” = the whole interval is inside ±δ_floor. k\* = 13 is the last grid point; “>13” = not reached. “Floor MSE / T1 floor term” = (noise_pred + noise_misfit)/noise_pred of `olb/ct_decomposition.csv` (MSE, as the 2.5 of Fig. 3d), independent of k; its bounds are the 90% group-bootstrap bounds of noise_misfit. “T1 gain” = T1's predicted gain of S6 over S0.
- **Table 4 (k\* rule):** a cell is khat [90% interval] / measured k\*; the interval is left out when it is one grid value; a dagger marks a measured k\* outside it (the dagger is the file's `k_emp_in_hat_ci`). The δ_floor rows use the split's own value (MTEB 1.17, 1.09, 1.06; Open LLM 1.20, 1.20 points). 9999 = not reached (>109 on MTEB, >13 on Open LLM). The rule is exploratory on MTEB (post hoc, PREREG Deviation 4a) and fixed in advance on Open LLM (Addendum 2).
- **Fig. 4 (rev. at d677557):** two Open LLM rows (G, S; their k grid ends at 13, so their k\* are not on the 109 scale of the MTEB hold-out rows) and the 10-task-type sweep row (N = 10, grid 1, 2, 3, 5; |C| = 7; post hoc grid, PREREG Deviation 4g) were added.
